1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,420
Young man: That seminar yesterday on automation

2
00:00:03,420 --> 00:00:06,930
and the future of work was really good, wasn't it?

3
00:00:06,930 --> 00:00:11,655
Looking at the first industrial revolution in Britain in the 19th century

4
00:00:11,655 --> 00:00:16,380
and seeing how people reacted to massive change was a real eye-opener.

5
00:00:16,380 --> 00:00:21,236
Young woman: Yes. It was interesting to hear how people felt about automation then

6
00:00:21,236 --> 00:00:23,180
and what challenges they faced

7
00:00:23,660 --> 00:00:27,480
I didn't know that first started with workers in the textile industry.

8
00:00:27,480 --> 00:00:32,284
Young man: With those protesting workers called the Luddites destroying their knitting machines

9
00:00:32,284 --> 00:00:35,610
because they were so worried about losing their jobs.

10
00:00:35,610 --> 00:00:39,752
Young woman: Yes, and ultimately, they didn't achieve anything.

11
00:00:39,752 --> 00:00:43,480
And anyway, industrialisation created more jobs than it destroyed.

12
00:00:43,480 --> 00:00:48,813
Young man: Yes, that's true - but it probably didn't seem a positive thing at the time.

13
00:00:48,813 --> 00:00:51,480
I can see why the Luddites felt so threatened.

14
00:00:51,480 --> 00:00:55,635
Young woman: I know. I'm sure I would have felt the same.

15
00:00:55,635 --> 00:00:59,790
The discussion about the future of work was really optimistic for a change.

16
00:00:59,790 --> 00:01:03,060
I like the idea that work won't involve doing boring

17
00:01:03,300 --> 00:01:06,766
repetitive tasks, as robots will do all that.

18
00:01:06,766 --> 00:01:10,070
Normally, you only hear negative stuff about the future.

19
00:01:10,070 --> 00:01:12,804
Young man: Bit too optimistic, don't you think?

20
00:01:12,804 --> 00:01:17,361
For example, I can't see how people are about to have more leisure time,

21
00:01:17,361 --> 00:01:21,310
when all the evidence shows people are spending longer than ever at work.

22
00:01:21,310 --> 00:01:25,120
Young woman: No - that's true. And what about lower unemployment

23
00:01:25,800 --> 00:01:27,200
I'm not so sure about that.

24
00:01:27,200 --> 00:01:31,410
Young man: Perhaps in the long term - but not in the foreseeable future.

25
00:01:31,410 --> 00:01:36,495
Young woman: Mmm. And I expect most people will be expected to work until they're much older -

26
00:01:36,495 --> 00:01:38,190
as everyone's living much longer.

27
00:01:38,190 --> 00:01:41,440
Young man: That's already happening.

28
00:01:41,440 --> 00:01:44,990
Young woman: I enjoyed all that stuff on how technology has changed some jobs

29
00:01:44,990 --> 00:01:47,780
and how they're likely to change in the near future.

30
00:01:47,780 --> 00:01:49,819
Young man: Yeah, incredible. Like accountants.

31
00:01:49,819 --> 00:01:54,915
You might think all the technological innovations would have put them out of a job,

32
00:01:54,915 --> 00:01:58,313
but in fact there are more of them than ever.

33
00:01:58,313 --> 00:02:02,390
They're still really in demand and have become far more efficient.

34
00:02:02,390 --> 00:02:04,630
Young woman: Right. That was amazing.

35
00:02:04,630 --> 00:02:09,110
Twenty times more accountants in this country compared to the 19th century.

36
00:02:09,110 --> 00:02:15,181
Young man: I know. I'd never have thought that demand for hairdressing would have gone up

37
00:02:15,181 --> 00:02:22,680
so much in the last hundred years. One hairdresser for every 287 people now, compared to one for over 1,500.

38
00:02:22,680 --> 00:02:25,779
Young woman: Yeah because people's earning power has gone up

39
00:02:25,779 --> 00:02:29,160
so they can afford to spend more on personal services like that.

40
00:02:29,160 --> 00:02:32,780
Young man: But technology hasn't changed the actual job that much.

41
00:02:32,780 --> 00:02:35,220
Young woman: No, they've got hairdryers, etc

42
00:02:35,560 --> 00:02:38,720
but it's one job where you don't depend on a computer...

43
00:02:38,720 --> 00:02:44,219
The kind of work that administrative staff do has changed enormously, thanks to technology.

44
00:02:44,219 --> 00:02:48,540
Even 20 years ago there were secretaries doing dictation and typing.

45
00:02:48,540 --> 00:02:51,813
Young man: Yes. Really boring compared to these days,

46
00:02:51,813 --> 00:02:55,450
when they're given much more responsibility and higher status.

47
00:02:55,450 --> 00:02:59,260
Young woman: Mmm. A lot of graduates go in for this kind of work now

48
00:02:59,580 --> 00:03:05,460
I'd expected there to be a much bigger change in the number of agricultural workers in the 19th century.

49
00:03:05,460 --> 00:03:11,840
But the 1871 census showed that roughly 25% of the population worked on the land.

50
00:03:11,840 --> 00:03:15,080
Young man: Yeah, I'd have assumed it would be more than 50

51
00:03:15,080 --> 00:03:17,570
Now it's less than 0.2%.

52
00:03:17,570 --> 00:03:19,700
Young woman: What about care workers?

53
00:03:19,700 --> 00:03:22,300
Young man: They barely existed in the 19th century

54
00:03:22,640 --> 00:03:25,392
as people's lifespan was so much shorter.

55
00:03:25,392 --> 00:03:28,740
But now of course this sector will see huge growth.

56
00:03:28,740 --> 00:03:31,700
Young woman: Yeah - and it's hard enough to meet current demand

57
00:03:33,020 --> 00:03:35,390
The future looks quite bleak for bank clerks.

58
00:03:35,390 --> 00:03:39,380
They've been in decline since ATMs were introduced in the eighties.

59
00:03:39,380 --> 00:03:45,156
Young man: And technology will certainly make most of the jobs they do now redundant, I think. Young woman:

60
00:03:45,156 --> 00:03:47,200
I agree, although the situation may change

61
00:03:47,800 --> 00:03:50,020
It's very hard to predict what will happen.
